@page
@model LearingWeb.Pages.AboutModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "About Son Heung-min — Son Heung-min Learning";
    var userName = HttpContext.Session.GetString("uname");
}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>@ViewData["Title"]</title>
    <link href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/bootstrap@5.2.3/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css" rel="stylesheet" />
    <style>
        body { font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; }
        .about-hero { background: #1b2a6b; color: white; padding: 40px 0; text-align: center; }
        .about-hero h1 { font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -.5px; }
        .about-body { padding: 20px 0 60px 0; }
        h4 { color: #1b2a6b; border-bottom: 2px solid #fb8a00; padding-bottom: 4px; }
        .timeline { padding-left: 20px; }
        .timeline li { margin-bottom: 10px; }
        .quote { font-size: 1.05rem; color: #495057; font-style: italic; border-left: 4px solid #fb8a00; padding-left: 16px; margin-top: 8px; }
        .footer-note { border-top: 1px solid #dee2e6; margin-top: 30px; padding: 20px 0; color: #6c757d; font-size: .85rem; text-align: center; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="about-hero">
        <div class="container">
            <h1>About Son Heung-min</h1>
            <p class="lead">The man, the journey, the English learner</p>
            <div style="margin-top: 12px;">
                @if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(userName))
                {
                    <a asp-page="/Login" class="btn btn-light me-2">Login</a>
                    <a asp-page="/Signin" class="btn btn-warning">Sign up</a>
                }
                else
                {
                    <span class="text-white">Hello, @userName!</span>
                    <form method="post" asp-page="/Logout" class="d-inline">
                        <button class="btn btn-light ms-2" type="submit">Logout</button>
                    </form>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="container about-body">
        <h4>Who is Son Heung-min?</h4>
        <p class="lead">
            Son Heung-min is a South Korean professional footballer who plays as a forward for the Los Angeles FC club
            and captains the South Korea national team. He is widely regarded as one of the best Asian footballers of all time.
        </p>

        <h4>Early Life — Chuncheon, South Korea</h4>
        <p>
            Son was born on 8 July 1992 in Chuncheon, Gangwon Province. His father, Son Woong-jung, was a former professional player
            who trained Son and his brother rigorously from a very young age — running along the beach, doing one-legged squats,
            and practicing ball control for hours every day. Son attended the Son Football Academy, which his father founded.
        </p>
        <p class="quote">
            "My father never wanted me to be a child who just plays football. He wanted me to be a real footballer."
        </p>

        <h4>FC Seoul Youth & Hamburg SV</h4>
        <p>
            At age 16, Son moved to Germany alone. He joined Hamburger SV, then Bayer Leverkusen.
            He learned German by watching SpongeBob SquarePants in German and through daily life in Germany.
            His breakthrough came in the 2012-2013 season at Leverkusen, when he scored on his Bundesliga debut.
        </p>

        <h4>Tottenham Hotspur — Ten Years at Spurs</h4>
        <p>
            Son joined Tottenham Hotspur in 2015 for a fee reported at around €22 million. He became the club's all-time
            top Asian scorer, won the Premier League Puskás Award in 2020 for a stunning solo goal against Bournemouth,
            and was named club captain in 2023. A first-team regular for nearly a decade, Son became the face of Spurs.
        </p>
        <p class="quote">
            "I want to be a good captain — to lead the team, to encourage all players, and to be a good example."
        </p>

        <h4>South Korea National Team & Captaincy</h4>
        <p>
            Son made his senior debut for the South Korea national team in 2010. He captained the side to a
            gold medal in football at the 2018 Asian Games — which granted him exemption from mandatory military service.
            Son has represented Korea at multiple FIFA World Cups, including 2014, 2018, and 2022.
        </p>
        <p class="quote">
            "I am very proud to be captain. I want to be a good captain — to lead the team, to encourage all players,
            to be a good example, to respect our fans, and to be a good ambassador for our country."
        </p>

        <h4>Language & Communication</h4>
        <p>
            Son is fluent in Korean, German, and English. Cambridge University Press even made a video about his language journey.
            In English interviews, Son speaks confidently — and he has said:
        </p>
        <p class="quote">
            "Of course you will make mistakes, but it's most important you try and then fix the problems. And please tell
            British people — or any English people — please speak a little bit more slowly, please."
        </p>

        <h4>Awards</h4>
        <ul class="timeline">
            <li>2020 — Premier League Puskás Award</li>
            <li>2020 — Asian Footballer of the Year (3rd time)</li>
            <li>2022 — Asian Games Gold Medal (as captain)</li>
            <li>2023 — Tottenham Hotspur Captain</li>
            <li>Multiple Premier League selections, AFC Asian Cup, FIFA World Cup appearances</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Philanthropy</h4>
        <p>
            Son established the Son Heung-min Scholarship Foundation to help young footballers in Korea.
            He has donated regularly to local communities, supported children's hospitals, and participated in charity campaigns
            both in Korea and internationally.
        </p>

        <h4>The Moment — Granting Military Exemption</h4>
        <p>
            At the 2018 Asian Games football final against Japan, Son scored the winning goal in the 90th minute —
            helping Korea win gold. It was a huge moment, because South Korea's top male athletes are required to serve
            in the military. Winning gold at the Asian Games exempted Son from that service, allowing him to focus on his club career.
        </p>

        <h4>2025 — Moving to Los Angeles FC</h4>
        <p>
            In 2025, Son moved to Los Angeles FC. His debut goal was an instant classic. He speaks about new challenges,
            teamwork, and his vision for the future of football. A new chapter begins in the United States.
        </p>

        <h4>Why this site exists</h4>
        <p>
            Son Heung-min Learning is an English learning website built around the real story of Son Heung-min.
            Every video, every story, and every certificate on this site is about Son — because when you learn English
            through a story you care about, learning becomes real.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="footer-note">
        © Son Heung-min Learning — 2026
    </div>
</body>
</html>
